Not all logo designers are the same. Knowing what to look for — and what questions to ask — will help you make the right choice rather than the cheapest one.

## Look at their portfolio, not their price

The most important thing to assess is the work they've already done. A logo designer's portfolio tells you more than any description of their process. Ask yourself:

- Do the logos look professional and well-crafted?
- Are they versatile — would they work in black and white, at small sizes?
- Is there range, or does everything look the same?
- Do they have experience in your industry or similar industries?

## Understand what's included

Logo design pricing varies widely — and so does what you get for your money. Always clarify:

- How many initial concepts will you receive?
- How many rounds of revisions are included?
- What file formats are delivered?
- Do you receive a brand identity (colour palette, fonts, guidelines) or just a logo file?
- Who owns the files after the project? (The answer should always be: you.)

## Be wary of very cheap options

Logo design services at very low price points — whether on freelancer platforms or automated online tools — typically produce generic results. You may end up with a logo that looks similar to dozens of other businesses, or a design that can't be properly adapted for print.

The difference between a £50 logo and a £200 logo isn't just quality — it's also whether you receive a complete brand identity, proper file formats, and a design that will hold up in the real world. Read more about [logo design on Fiverr vs. a professional designer](/blog/logo-design-fiverr-vs-professional-designer/) for a more detailed comparison.

## Communication matters

Regardless of the designer's skill level, you need to be able to communicate clearly. Look for someone who asks you questions, explains their thinking, and responds reliably.

